    <build>
        <pluginManagement>
            <plugins>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                    <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${compiler.plugin.version}</version>
                </plugin>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                    <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${surefire.version}</version>
                    <configuration>
                        <!--
                          #294: a test that hangs is worse than a test that fails, because
                          what it leaves behind is not a result. The fork stops speaking,
                          surefire waits, and whatever finally kills the JVM leaves

                            The forked VM terminated without properly saying goodbye
                            Process Exit Code: 143

                          Reproduced deliberately, and the reactor summary that comes
                          with it is worse than #294 records. It is not that there is no
                          `Tests run:` line — surefire still prints

                            [INFO] Tests run: 0, Failures: 0, Errors: 0, Skipped: 0

                          It is that the per-class line is missing and the summary reads
                          zero failures. A harness told to refuse anything with no
                          `Tests run:` line therefore accepts this, and a harness scoring
                          on the build's exit status records it as a kill. Either way a
                          hang does not merely break a build — it manufactures false
                          evidence in the one technique this repository uses to decide
                          whether a test earns its place.

                          A default deadline is the fix, because it applies to the 177 of
                          188 test classes that carry no @Timeout of their own and to
                          every test written after this line. An explicit @Timeout on a
                          class or method still wins, so nothing already tuned — including
                          MemoryRaceTest's deliberate 150s — is touched.

                          Measured end to end on this JDK (21.0.10), agentkit-json, one
                          module, a test parked in an uninterruptible socket read:

                            origin/main   still hung at 421s when the run was killed,
                                          no `Tests run:` line for the class at all
                            with this     Tests run: 1, Errors: 1 at 300.1s, and the
                                          report carries the stuck thread's own stack —
                                          down through SocketInputStream.read to the
                                          exact line of the test that parked there

                          The second is a defect anybody can act on. The first is the
                          thing #294 is about.

                          Five minutes, and the number comes from the reactor rather than
                          from taste. Across 2,267 test methods on a full `clean verify`,
                          the slowest method that does NOT already carry its own @Timeout
                          is the one in AgentGraphTest that shuts an executor down under a
                          running graph, at 5.30s; next is 3.27s. Five minutes is 57x
                          that. The
                          headroom is not idle: MemoryRaceTest records that under a loaded
                          machine its own slowest run reached 109.7s against a 150s bound,
                          so this reactor is known to run twenty times slower under
                          contention — which is exactly the condition #294's sightings
                          were all seen under. It is a ceiling, not a budget: it costs
                          nothing on a healthy run, and a hang now costs five minutes and
                          a named failure instead of a build that never ends.

                          SEPARATE_THREAD, not the JUnit default of SAME_THREAD. In
                          SAME_THREAD mode JUnit can only interrupt the test thread, which
                          releases a latch or a join but NOT an uninterruptible blocking
                          read; measured against a test parked in Socket.read, SAME_THREAD
                          left the build hung past 150s while SEPARATE_THREAD reported
                          `Tests run: 1, Errors: 1` in 25s naming the stuck thread. That
                          is the difference between a bounded failure and a wedge, and the
                          shapes this repository actually blocks on — sockets in the
                          openrouter and itops tests, the pipe to an MCP subprocess — are
                          exactly the uninterruptible ones. It is also what the two test
                          classes here that thought hardest about hangs already choose by
                          hand: MemoryRaceTest and SkillRaceTest both spell
                          threadMode = SEPARATE_THREAD on their own @Timeout.

                          Rejected: <forkedProcessTimeoutInSeconds> as the primary guard.
                          Measured twice, it did NOT release a fork parked in an
                          uninterruptible read: with the timeout set to 25s the build was
                          still hung at 100s on one attempt and at 401s on the other, and
                          surefire printed no timeout message either time. It closes the
                          command channel, the fork sees EOF and tries to exit, and
                          cannot. It is left where agentkit-pentest already sets it, and
                          left alone: the one shape it might still cover is a hang
                          OUTSIDE a test method, where JUnit's deadline cannot see
                          anything — and that was not measured either way here, so it is
                          neither claimed nor removed.

                          Rejected: a JUnit extension that fails a test leaving an
                          HttpClient operation in flight, which is what #294 asks for.
                          Measured on this JDK, an operation in flight is harmless — every
                          thread an HttpClient starts is a daemon thread, and a surefire
                          fork that left a stalled stream running still reported
                          `Tests run: 1` and BUILD SUCCESS. See JdkHttpTransport's `http`
                          field for the measurements and for the thing that does hang.
                        -->
                        <properties>
                            <configurationParameters>
                                junit.jupiter.execution.timeout.default = 300 s
                                junit.jupiter.execution.timeout.thread.mode.default = SEPARATE_THREAD
                            </configurationParameters>
                        </properties>
                    </configuration>
                </plugin>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                    <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${enforcer.plugin.version}</version>
                </plugin>
                <plugin>
                    <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                    <artifactId>maven-javadoc-plugin</artifactId>
                    <version>${javadoc.plugin.version}</version>
                    <configuration>
                        <!--
                          What makes `mvn javadoc:javadoc` a real check: a {@link} that
                          no longer resolves, a block tag where it is not allowed, and
                          malformed HTML all fail the build. Each verified by introducing
                          one and watching it fail.

                          The groups are listed out rather than written as `all,-missing`.
                          That spelling — and -Xdoclint:all,-missing passed through
                          additionalJOption — switches doclint off altogether, so the
                          check silently checks nothing.

                          `missing` is excluded on purpose: turning it on makes ~100
                          absent @param/@return across the modules into build failures.
                          That is a documentation backlog, not breakage. Add it if you
                          ever want to work it down.

                          One gap: a doc comment orphaned by a second comment below it
                          (which silently un-documents the member) needs JDK 22+ to
                          detect, so it slips through here. It has bitten this repo once.
                        -->
                        <doclint>reference,syntax,html,accessibility</doclint>
                        <notimestamp>true</notimestamp>
                    </configuration>
                </plugin>
            </plugins>
        </pluginManagement>
        <plugins>
            <!--
              Bound to the lifecycle rather than run as a separate `javadoc:javadoc`
              invocation. Standalone, the goal resolves inter-module dependencies from the
              local repository instead of the reactor, so it fails on a machine that has
              not installed the modules — which is every clean CI runner, and was not my
              machine, so this passed locally and failed in CI.
            -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-javadoc-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>check-javadoc</id>
                        <phase>verify</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>javadoc</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-enforcer-plugin</artifactId>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>enforce-toolchain</id>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>enforce</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <rules>
                                <requireMavenVersion>
                                    <version>[${min.maven.version},)</version>
                                </requireMavenVersion>
                                <requireJavaVersion>
                                    <version>[${min.java.version},)</version>
                                </requireJavaVersion>
                            </rules>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
